text = "[font_size=20]How Filters work[/font_size]
Pegleg uses Filter inputs in multiple places, but primarily they're used for searching through lists of searchable elements such as items or missions.

[font_size=16][u]Search Tags[/u][/font_size]
Searchable elements have an internal list of Search Tags generated by PegLeg. For example, a Weapon or Schematic may have a [code]Copper[/code] or [code]Malachite[/code] tag, and a Hero may have a [code]Constructor[/code] tag.

By default, Filters treat text as case-insensitive partial matches of search tags, separated by spaces, in which all matches must pass on an element for it to be filtered. Each of these matches is a form of Condition.
A filter of [code]Schematic legEND cyth[/code] would show only Legendary Scythe Schematics.
[color=dark_gray][font_size=12]Note that [code][font_size=11]legEND[/font_size][/code] was able to match with the [code][font_size=11]Legendary[/font_size][/code] tag even with mismatching capitalisation, and that [code][font_size=11]pear[/font_size][/code] was able to match with the [code][font_size=11]Spear[/font_size][/code] tag even when it only contained letters in the middle of the tag.[/font_size][/color]

[font_size=16][u]Operators[/u][/font_size]
By default, all Conditions in a Filter must be true for an item to be filtered. The [code]![/code] and [code]|[/code] Oparators can control this behavior. 

Prefixing a Condition with [code]![/code] will produce an opposite result 
([code]Schematic !Legendary[/code] would filter non-Legendary Schematics)

Placing a [code]|[/code] between two conditions will make both pass if either were true. This operator can be chained with itself to work on any amount of conditions.
([code]Rare | Epic | Legendary[/code] would filter any Rare, Epic, or Legendary items)

[font_size=16][u]Expressions[/u][/font_size]
In some cases you may want more complex filters that only check some conditions when other conditions have been met. This can be achieved with Expressions, which allow you to merge multiple Conditions into a single Condition

Encasing a set of conditions inside [code]()[/code] brackets will evaluate the conditions as if they were a separate Filter, and then use the result of that Filter as the result of a Condition.
([code]Schematic | (Survivor Epic)[/code] would filter any Schematics or Epic Survivors)

[font_size=16][u]Precise Search Tag Matching[/u][/font_size]
Partial matching of Search Tags fits most use cases, but some scenarios require more specificity. For example, searching for [code]Explosive[/code] would filter Explosive weapons, but it would also filter Ammo: Explosive, and the Schematic that crafts it.

Encasing text in [code]\"[/code] quotation marks transforms it into an Exact Search Tag. Unlike standard Search Tags, these can conatin spaces, and will only pass if an element contains a search tag that completely matches the enclosed text, though it remins case insensitive.
([code]\"b.a.s.e. kyle\"[/code] would filter B.A.S.E Kyle, but not Heavy Base Kyle)

[font_size=16][u]Advanced Search Conditions[/u][/font_size]
Advanced Search Conditions allow you to query specific data from search elements. This is useful for numeric queries such as mission power level or item quantity, and for querying specific properties of an element such as their tier or level.
[color=dark_gray][font_size=12]This Condition type is admittedly hard to utilise, due to the difficulty in determining what properties an item has, and it is reccomended to only use this if you know what you're doing[/font_size][/color]

Advanced Search Queries utilise a [code]propertyPath[/code], which points to a json property of an element. Elements often contain properties nested within other properties, which can be accessed with a [code].[/code] full stop. 
For a [code]level[/code] property nested inside an [code]attributes[/code] property, the path would be [code]attributes.level[/code]

Some properties may represent a list of nested properties. You can evaluate your query against any property in the list by querying the list itself, or you can evaluate a specific element in a list by using a number as a subproperty ([code]list.0[/code]). You can previx the number with [code]^[/code] to count backwards from the end of a list instead of forwards from the beginning ([code]list.^1[/code])

A text query is formatted as [code]propertyPath=text[/code] and functions the same as a Search Tag condition, but only against that specific property. 
It also supports Exact values, such as 
[code]propertyPath=\"exact value\"[/code]

A numeric query can be formatted as:
[ul]
[code]propertyPath=2[/code] matches an exact value
[code]propertyPath=2..[/code] matches greater or equal values
[code]propertyPath=..2[/code] matches less or equal values
[code]propertyPath=1..3[/code] matches values in between
[/ul]"
fit_content = true
scroll_active = false
